nchors and simultaneously changes the direction cue:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:16:#   the ray must follow the arrow's protruding head, not the side where the seed happens to touch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:18:#   Example: a right-facing arrow may have its seed above or below it, but the output still repeats that seed to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:22:# - Arrow templates are limited to a few reusable families so humans can learn \"the protruding tip points the ray\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:24:# - Each grid uses at least two arrow orientations, so the shape cue is actually demonstrated inside one sample.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:26:# - `detect_dominant_big_component_color()` still works because arrows remain the only components with bbox >= 3x3.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:31:# - The generator is output-first: place full arrows and full rays first, then erase all but one long clue ray.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:32:# - Arrows, rays, and their 4-neighborhood guards are kept separate so two non-arrow components never merge under\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:33:#   `label_mask((grid != bg) & (grid != arrow_color))`.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:34:# - Candidate seeds are rejected if they go out of bounds, overlap, or even touch previously committed rays / arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:36:# - The crucial cue is human-visible geometry: each arrow has one protruding tip and that tip points the repetition.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:38:# - The kept pre-extended clue ray shows that the repeated payload follows the arrow orientation rather than contact.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/53fb4810/4/code.py:4