p/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:20:# - The moving marker is no longer a 1-cell-thick line. It is a fixed 3x3 arrow sprite:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:25:# - The destination marker is an identical arrow sprite of the same color, translated further\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:26:#   along the arrow's pointing direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:27:# - To make \"direction from shape\" necessary, the arrow is attached only to a side orthogonal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:32:#   Variant logic: an arrow attached above may still point right, so the container moves right.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:33:#   The solver must read the arrow shape, then find the matching arrow destination along that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:37:# - The arrow has only four orientations and one fixed size, so the new degree of freedom is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:47:#   the subset of pixels closest to the border. With an arrow sprite, that \"closest layer only\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:48:#   extraction would lose most of the arrow and break matching.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:50:#   shape, matches it against four fixed arrow templates, and derives direction from that match.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:53:# - Each generated object reserves a rectangle covering: input container, input arrow, destination\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:54:#   arrow, moved container, and moved arrow. Reserved rectangles are packed with a 1-cell margin,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:56:# - The destination arrow is always at least one background cell away from the head arrow, which\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/271d71e2/D4/2/code.py:60:# - Because the arrow 